Note that  $Y$  and  $Z$  have the same distribution and  $X_0$  and  $Z$  are independent.
Assume for the sake of contradiction that the distribution function of  $Y$  is not continuous.

Denote by  $a_1, a_2, \dots a_k$  the points for which  $$ P(Y=x_i)= \max_{a} \{P(Y=a)\} =: p. $$   
Now, we have that
\begin{align*}
p = P(Y=a_i)=P(X_0+\alpha Z = a_i) &= \sum_{P(X_0=x)>0}P(X_0=x)P(Z=\frac{a_i-x}{\alpha})
& \leqslant \sum_{P(X_0=x)>0}P(X_0=x)\max_{j} \left \{P(Z=\frac{a_j-x}{\alpha}) \right \} \leqslant p.
\end{align*}
So we must have equality everywhere, which implies that   $$  P(Z=\frac{a_k-x}{\alpha}) = p \quad \text{if} \quad P(X_0=x) > 0 $$  Now take
\begin{align*}
P_a =   & \{x : P(Z=\frac{a-x}{\alpha})= p \}
T =  &\{ x : P(X_0=x)> 0 \}.
\end{align*}
Then  $$ T \subseteq \cap_{i=1}^{n}P_{a_i} = \cap_{i=1}^{n} (P_0 + a_i) $$  Therefore   $$ T - a_i \subseteq P_0 \quad , \quad i = 1,2,\dots, n. $$   
